L000023	\title{Comment on ``Evolution Operator Can Always Be Separated into the Product of Holonomy and Dynamic Operators''}
L000024	
L000025	\author{Adam Fredriksson}
L000026	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Astronomy, Uppsala University, Box 516, SE-751 20 Uppsala, Sweden}
L000027	
L000028	\author{Erik Sj\"oqvist}
L000029	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Astronomy, Uppsala University,
L000030	Box 516, SE-751 20 Uppsala, Sweden}
L000031	\email{erik.sjoqvist@physics.uu.se}
L000032	
L000033	\date{\today}
L000034	
L000035	\maketitle
L000036	
L000037	In a recent Letter \cite{yu23}, Yu and Tong derived an expression for a generic time
L000038	evolution operator acting on a subspace, claimed to constitute a general separation
L000039	into a product of a holonomy operator and a dynamic operator. This result appears
L000040	to be in conflict with prior work by Anandan \cite{anandan88}, in which the time
L000041	evolution was found not to separate into a holonomic and a dynamic part in general.
L000042	In this Comment, we show that the claim in Ref.~\cite{yu23} that the time evolution
L000043	operator always can be written as a product of a holonomy operator and a dynamic
L000044	operator is false, as it is based on a circular use of the time evolution operator.
L000045	
L000046	The key finding of Ref.~\cite{yu23} is that given a Hilbert space $\mathscr{H}$ of some
L000047	quantum system and the $\ell$ dimensional ($\ell \leq \dim \mathscr{H}$) subspace
L000048	spanned by $\{\ket{\psi_{j}(t)}\}_{j = 1}^{\ell}$, where each $\ket{\psi_{j}(t)}$ is a solution
L000049	of the Schr\"odinger equation with Hamiltonian $H(t)$, the time evolution operator
L000050	$U(t,0) = \ket{\psi_{j}(t)}\bra{\psi_{j}(0)}$ satisfies the differential equation
L000051	\begin{eqnarray}
L000052	\dot{U}(t,0) = \dot{P}(t) U(t,0) + U(t,0) F(t,0),
L000053	\label{eq:yte}
L000054	\end{eqnarray}
L000055	where $P(t)\equiv\ket{\psi_{j}(t)}\bra{\psi_{j}(t)}$ is the projector on the subspace,
L000056	$F(t,0) \equiv F_{jk}(t)\ket{\psi_{j}(0)}\bra{\psi_{k} (0)}$ with
L000057	$F_{jk}(t)\equiv -i\bra{\psi_{j}(t)} H(t)\ket{\psi_{k} (t)}$, we use Einstein's summation
L000058	convention, i.e., repeated indices are implicitly summed, and we put $\hbar = 1$.
L000059	Formally, Eq.~\eqref{eq:yte} implies that
L000060	\begin{eqnarray}
L000061	U(t,0) & = &
L000062	{\mathcal{P}}e^{\int_0^t \dot{P} (\tau) d\tau}
L000063	P(0) \bar{\mathcal{T}} e ^{\int_{0}^{t}  F(\tau,0) d\tau} ,
L000064	\label{eq:yte_solution}
L000065	\end{eqnarray}
L000066	which is the expression for $U(t,0)$ found in Ref.~\cite{yu23} that was claimed to
L000067	imply a separation into a product of a holonomy operator and a dynamic operator,
L000068	given by a path ordered exponential  ${\mathcal{P}}e^{\int_0^{t} \dot{P} (\tau) d\tau} P(0)$
L000069	and a reverse time ordered exponential $P(0)\bar{\mathcal{T}} e ^{\int_{0}^{t}  F(\tau,0) d\tau}$,
L000070	respectively.
L000071	
L000072	To understand why Eq.~\eqref{eq:yte_solution} does not imply the claimed separation,
L000073	one should note that $F(t,0)$ in fact depends on the time evolution operator itself:
L000074	\begin{eqnarray}
L000075	F(t,0) & = & -i\ket{\psi_j (0)} \bra{\psi_{j}(t)} H(t)\ket{\psi_{k} (t)} \bra{\psi_{k} (0)}
L000076	\nonumber \\
L000077	 & = & -iU^{\dagger}(t,0)H(t)U(t,0) .
L000078	 \label{eq:F}
L000079	\end{eqnarray}
L000080	This observation, which seems to be overlooked in Ref.~\cite{yu23}, allows us to
L000081	write Eq.~\eqref{eq:yte_solution} as
L000082	\begin{eqnarray}
L000083	U(t,0) =
L000084	\mathcal{P}e^{\int_0^{t} \dot{P} (\tau) d\tau} P (0)
L000085	\bar{\mathcal{T}}
L000086	e ^{-i\int_{0}^{t} U^{\dagger} (\tau,0) H(\tau) U(\tau,0) d\tau} .
L000087	\nonumber \\
L000088	\label{eq:ytesol}
L000089	\end{eqnarray}
L000090	Evidently, the `dynamic' operator, the last factor on the right-hand side, contains
L000091	contributions from the holonomy operator via $U(t,0)$ in a circular manner. In other
L000092	words, if one insists on calling Eq.~\eqref{eq:yte_solution} a separation into a product
L000093	of a holonomy operator and a dynamic operator, it follows, by Eq.~\eqref{eq:ytesol},
L000094	that the `dynamic' operator depends non-trivially on the holonomy operator. Thus,
L000095	contrary to the claim in Ref.~\cite{yu23}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:yte_solution} does not
L000096	constitute a general separation of $U(t,0)$ into a holonomy operator and dynamic
L000097	operator.
L000098	
